{
  "root_cause": "Pikaday's global keydown listener prevents default for arrow keys whenever the calendar is visible, regardless of which element has focus, trapping arrow keys and preventing caret movement in other inputs.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify _onKeyChange to only handle arrow (and enter/escape) keys when the calendar is visible and the associated input field (or trigger) has focus, preserving date picker navigation when appropriate while allowing normal text field editing.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "pikaday.js",
      "sha256":"662123ec9f429dee9cffaacfcf89b05ed9c3c30a78d33ecfd113e6778349cc77",
      "before":"        self._onKeyChange = function(e)
        {
            e = e || window.event;

            if (self.isVisible()) {

                switch(e.keyCode){
                    case 13:
                    case 27:
                        if (opts.field) {
                            opts.field.blur();
                        }
                        break;
                    case 37:
                        e.preventDefault();
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 1);
                        break;
                    case 38:
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 7);
                        break;
                    case 39:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 1);
                        break;
                    case 40:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 7);
                        break;
                }
            }
        }",
      "after":"        self._onKeyChange = function(e)
        {
            e = e || window.event;

            if (self.isVisible() && opts.field && document.activeElement === opts.field) {

                switch(e.keyCode){
                    case 13:
                    case 27:
                        if (opts.field) {
                            opts.field.blur();
                        }
                        break;
                    case 37:
                        e.preventDefault();
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 1);
                        break;
                    case 38:
                        self.adjustDate('subtract', 7);
                        break;
                    case 39:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 1);
                        break;
                    case 40:
                        self.adjustDate('add', 7);
                        break;
                }
            }
        }"
    }
  ],
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Arrow keys will now function as expected in other text fields when the date picker is visible, allowing users to correct text without moving focus inadvertently; keyboard navigation of the date picker remains functional when the field has focus.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "If the picker is bound to a trigger element separate from the field, arrow keys may not work when the trigger has focus.",
    "If opts.field is null, no keyboard navigation will occur (unlikely in typical usage).",
    "Enter and Escape to blur the field will only occur when the field has focus, which is acceptable."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Test that ArrowLeft in the notes field moves the caret and allows inserting a space to produce "Gate 4".",
    "Verify that arrow keys still change the date when the delivery-date field has focus and the calendar is open.",
    "Ensure Enter and Esc still close the picker and blur the field when appropriate.",
    "Confirm no regression in other keyboard navigation (e.g., PageUp/Down) within the date picker.",
    "Validate behavior with both bound:true and bound:false configurations."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Journey steps:
1. seek selector "#notes"
2. type "Gate4"
3. press ArrowLeft
4. type " "

Assertion after step 4 expects notes field value ["Gate 4"] but observed was ["Gate4 "] meaning after typing Gate4 then pressing ArrowLeft then space resulted in "Gate4 " instead of "Gate 4". So the issue: When focusing on the notes input, pressing ArrowLeft moved the caret? Actually we typed Gate4, then pressed ArrowLeft (which likely moved caret left within the input?), then typed space resulting in insertion before the 4? Let's simulate: Starting empty, type G a t e 4 -> "Gate4". Caret after the 4. Press ArrowLeft moves caret left one character, between 'e' and '4'. Then typing space inserts space there, yielding "Gate 4"? Wait that would produce "Gate 4"? Actually inserting space between e and 4 yields "Gate 4"? Let's check: characters: G a t e 4. Insert space between e and 4 gives "Gate 4". That matches expected. But observed got "Gate4 " meaning space appended at end, implying ArrowLeft didn't move caret; maybe it triggered something else like moving focus to previous element (like the date field) causing the space to be typed elsewhere? However assertion checks notes field value, so space ended up in notes field at end. Means ArrowLeft didn't affect caret position inside notes; perhaps ArrowLeft was captured by the date picker (Pikaday) and caused some action that prevented default? In observed keyEvents list we see:

keyEvents:
[
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"Tab","target":"body","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"Tab","target":"#delivery-date","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"G","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"a","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"t","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"e","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":"4","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":true,"key":"ArrowLeft","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"},
 {"defaultPrevented":false,"key":" ","target":"#notes","type":"keydown"}
]

So ArrowLeft keydown had defaultPrevented:true, meaning something prevented default action (likely the Pikaday calendar's key handling). Indeed Pikaday has _onKeyChange listener on document keydown when keyboardInput:true. It intercepts arrow keys to adjust date when calendar is visible. If calendar is visible, ArrowLeft triggers adjustDate('subtract',1) and prevents default. That prevents caret movement in the notes input, causing space to be appended at end.

Thus the bug: When the calendar is visible, Pikaday's global keydown listener prevents default for arrow keys even when focus is not in the date field but in another input, because it listens on document and checks if self.isVisible() (calendar visible). It does not check whether the target is the field associated with the picker. So arrow keys are swallowed globally whenever calendar is open, regardless of focus.

Fix: In _onKeyChange, we should only handle arrow keys if the event target is the picker's field (or maybe if the picker is bound and the trigger is the field). According to Pikaday code: they add listener on document keydown if opts.keyboardInput true. Inside _onKeyChange they check if self.isVisible() then handle arrow keys. They should also check that event.target is the associated input (opts.field) or maybe that the picker is bound and the trigger is the field. Since bound:false in this usage (they set bound: false), but they still attached listener to document. The condition should also verify that the active element is the field (or that the picker's field is the one that triggered opening?). Usually Pikaday's keyboardInput is meant to work when the picker is bound to an input; they check if opts.field and document.activeElement === opts.field? Let's look at the source: In _onKeyChange they have:

if (self.isVisible()) {
    switch(e.keyCode){
        case 13:
        case 27:
            if (opts.field) {
                opts.field.blur();
            }
            break;
        case 37:
            e.preventDefault();
            self.adjustDate('subtract', 1);
            break;
        case 38:
            self.adjustDate('subtract', 7);
            break;
        case 39:
            self.adjustDate('add', 1);
            break;
        case 40:
            self.adjustDate('add', 7);
            break;
    }
}

Thus they don't check target. To fix, we need to add a condition that the event.target is opts.field (or maybe opts.trigger) when bound, or more generally that the picker's field is the one that has focus (or that the picker is bound and the trigger is the field). Since bound:false here, but they still want arrow keys to work when focus is on the field? Actually they set bound:false, meaning they manually manage showing/hiding? They attached picker to field and container but bound:false means they don't attach focus/blur listeners to trigger; they manually call show/hide? In the code they didn't call show/hide; they just constructed picker with field and container, bound:false. The picker likely shows on field focus? Let's see: In constructor, if opts.field and !opts.container and !opts.bound they insert before field.nextSibling and add listeners for change etc. They also set defaultDate. They do not automatically show on focus unless bound:true. However they might rely on user clicking field to show? Actually they didn't attach click/focus listeners because bound:false. So how does calendar appear? Probably they call picker.show() elsewhere? Not in given code. Maybe the calendar is always shown because they appended to container and never hide? Actually they call this.show() if opts.bound? In constructor: if (opts.bound) { this.hide(); ... } else { this.show(); }. So if bound:false, they call this.show() immediately, making calendar visible always. Indeed that matches scenario: calendar is visible on page load. So the calendar is always visible, thus key listener intercepts arrow keys globally.

Goal: While calendar is on the page, ArrowLeft in another text field still moves the caret so the user can fix their text. So we need to modify Pikaday to only intercept arrow keys when the picker is bound to the field and the field has focus (or when the picker is bound and the trigger is the field). For bound:false case, we probably shouldn't intercept arrow keys at all because calendar is always visible but not attached to an input; but they may still want arrow keys to navigate calendar when focused? Actually if bound:false and calendar always visible, maybe they intend arrow keys to work when calendar has focus? But calendar is not focusable (it's a div). So likely they never intended arrow keys to do anything when bound:false. So fix: In _onKeyChange, only process arrow keys if opts.field && document.activeElement === opts.field (or maybe opts.trigger). Also for bound case, they already have logic to show/hide on focus/blur; but they also set aria-label on field when bound. So we can check if opts.field && document.activeElement === opts.field.

